# Responsive Design: One Site, Every Screen

> A phone is 360 CSS pixels wide; a desktop monitor, 1920 or more.
> Building separate mobile and desktop sites died a decade ago — the
> modern answer is **responsive design**: one flexible layout that
> reflows itself to fit any screen, the way water takes the shape of
> its glass.

## 1. The Mobile-First Mindset

[definition]
**Responsive web design** (Ethan Marcotte, 2010) = **fluid grids +
flexible media + media queries** — a single HTML document whose
layout adapts to the viewport instead of maintaining separate mobile
and desktop pages.
[/definition]

Over half of web traffic is phones; Google indexes **mobile-first**.
So the workflow inverts: design the narrow layout first, then
layer on enhancements (`min-width` media queries) as space grows.
Starting wide and "shrinking down" tends to leave cramped
afterthoughts.

## 2. The Viewport: Where Geometry Begins

```html
<meta name="viewport"
      content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
```

Without this meta tag, phones pretend to be 980 px wide and zoom
out — your media queries never fire as intended. With it, the
browser's CSS width equals the real device width, and 1 CSS pixel
maps predictably to physical pixels.

## 3. Fluid Units: The End of Fixed Pixels

Fixed widths (`width: 960px`) break the moment the screen is 360 px.
Fluid units adapt:

| Unit | Meaning | Use for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `%` | Relative to parent | Column widths |
| `em` / `rem` | Relative to font size (rem = root) | Spacing, type scale |
| `vw` / `vh` | 1% of viewport width / height | Hero sections |
| `fr` | Fraction of free space (grid) | Track sizing |
| `clamp(min, ideal, max)` | Fluid value with bounds | Font sizes, padding |

```css
/* Grows smoothly 1rem → 1.25rem between 360px and 1200px viewports */
h1 { font-size: clamp(1rem, 0.8rem + 1vw, 1.25rem); }

/* Container breathes with the screen */
.container { width: min(100% - 2rem, 68rem); margin-inline: auto; }
```

Flexible media round out the technique:

```css
img, video { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }
```

## 4. Media Queries: Layout Checkpoints

```css
/* Base styles = mobile. Enhance upward: */
.card-grid { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }

@media (min-width: 600px) {
  .card-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
}
@media (min-width: 1000px) {
  .card-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); }
}
```

[important]
Query by **content behaviour**, not device names. "iPhone 14" widths
change yearly; "the point where two columns start feeling cramped"
is stable. Common breakpoints — small < 600, medium 600–1000, large
> 1000 CSS px — are conventions, not laws. And there are no rules
at all without the viewport meta tag: it is the foundation media
queries stand on.
[/important]

## 5. Modern Layout: Flexbox and Grid

Two systems now do the heavy lifting, replacing float hacks:

- **Flexbox** — one dimension: rows *or* columns. Toolbars, nav
  bars, cards in a strip.

```css
.nav { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.75rem; }
.nav .spacer { margin-left: auto; }   /* pushes the rest right */
```

- **Grid** — two dimensions: rows *and* columns, with named areas
  that redefine themselves per breakpoint:

```css
.layout { display: grid;
  grid-template-areas: "nav" "main" "aside"; }
@media (min-width: 900px) {
  .layout { grid-template-areas: "nav nav" "aside main";
            grid-template-columns: 260px 1fr; }
}
```

The `auto-fill` + `minmax` combo builds layouts that need **no media
queries at all** — the grid simply fits as many cards as the width
allows:

```css
.cards { display: grid;
  grid-template-columns:
    repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 16rem), 1fr)); }
```

@diagram flowchart id="responsive-decisions" layout="hierarchical" aria="Choosing a responsive strategy"
  node fluid label="Fluid units (%, rem, clamp, vw)" shape="round-rect"
  node flex label="Flexbox — 1D rows/strips" shape="round-rect"
  node grid label="Grid — 2D areas" shape="round-rect"
  node mq label="Media queries — content checkpoints" shape="diamond"

  fluid -> flex
  fluid -> grid
  flex -> mq
  grid -> mq
@/diagram

## 6. Images and Media That Fit

- **`srcset` and `sizes`** let the browser pick a photo sized to the
  viewport — no 3000 px download on a phone:

```html
<img src="hero-800.jpg"
     srcset="hero-400.jpg 400w, hero-800.jpg 800w, hero-1600.jpg 1600w"
     sizes="(min-width: 900px) 60vw, 100vw"
     alt="Sunrise over the mountains">
```

- **`<picture>`** swaps crops (a wide hero on desktop, a tight
  portrait on phones) and serves modern formats with fallback.
- Icons favour SVG — resolution-independent and tiny.

## 7. Beyond Width: A Responsive Checklist

- **Touch targets** ≥ 44 × 44 px; hover must never be the only way
  to reach a function.
- **Typography**: body ≥ 16 px; line length 45–75 characters.
- **Performance**: phones are on slow networks — lazy-load images
  (`loading="lazy"`), and prefer fewer, smaller assets. Speed is a
  responsive feature.
- **Test** on real devices and by resizing DevTools; check landscape
  and zoom to 200% (an accessibility requirement).
- **Dark mode** via `prefers-color-scheme`, reduced motion via
  `prefers-reduced-motion` — media queries ask preferences, not just
  width.

[summary]
Responsive design = one document, many layouts: the viewport meta
tag, fluid units (%, rem, vw, clamp), and flexible media form the
base; media queries at content-driven breakpoints reflow it;
flexbox handles one-dimensional strips and grid handles
two-dimensional areas — often with no breakpoints at all via
auto-fill + minmax. Serve right-sized images with srcset, keep
touch targets large, and treat performance and user preferences
(prefers-color-scheme, reduced motion) as part of responsiveness.
[/summary]
